<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bega Canal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bega Canal is a navigable canal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which follows and regulates the course of the Bega river. It was dug from 1728 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-florimund-mercy">Claudius Florimund Mercy</a> to drain marshes and link <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeswar</a>, now Timișoara, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, and it carried goods to the city for two centuries. It passes through Timișoara and Zrenjanin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bega-canal.jpg" alt="Bega Canal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miomir Magdevski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bega_Canal_(kanal_Begeja)_u_Temi%C5%A1varu_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
